---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import MoreInCategory from "../components/MoreInCategory.astro";
import { authorSchema, authorName } from "../data/authors";
import AuthorAvatar from "../components/AuthorAvatar.astro";
import QuickPicks from "../components/QuickPicks.astro";
import PlanTheJob from "../components/PlanTheJob.astro";
import AuthorBio from "../components/AuthorBio.astro";
import { amazonLink } from "../data/affiliate";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";
import { pageDates } from "../data/dates";

const moreInCategory = [
  {
    "title": "Best String Trimmers",
    "url": "/best-string-trimmer/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Weed Eaters",
    "url": "/best-weed-eater/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Lawn Edgers",
    "url": "/best-lawn-edger/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Hedge Trimmers",
    "url": "/best-hedge-trimmer/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Cordless Leaf Blowers",
    "url": "/best-cordless-leaf-blower/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Battery-Powered Lawn Mowers",
    "url": "/best-battery-powered-lawn-mower/"
  }
];
const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-10-17", modified: "2026-10-17" });
const title = "Best Battery String Trimmers: 7 Picks by Battery Platform";
const description =
  "Battery string trimmers compared by platform, watt-hours, line size and attachments, so you buy the cordless trimmer that suits the batteries you already own.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    "id": "ego-st1623t",
    "name": "EGO Power+ ST1623T 16-Inch Line IQ Powerload String Trimmer Kit",
    "badge": "Best Overall",
    "category": "56V, Brushless, 16-Inch Swath, Auto-Feed Head, 4.0Ah Kit",
    "description": "EGO's ST1623T is the trimmer to beat if you are starting fresh or already run EGO's 56V mower or blower. Two head features do most of the work. Powerload winds new line onto the spool at the press of a button, so you feed a length of line through the head and let the motor wrap it, and Line IQ senses line length and feeds it automatically while you cut, so there is no bumping. EGO lists a 16-inch swath, 0.095-inch twisted line, a brushless motor and a telescopic carbon fiber shaft, and the kit ships with a 4.0Ah battery and EGO's 320W charger. EGO rates it for up to 60 minutes of continuous cutting on that pack. At 56 volts and 4.0 amp-hours, the included battery carries more stored energy than any other kit battery on this list, which is why runtime is rarely the complaint owners raise. The trade-offs are that it is a dedicated trimmer, not an attachment powerhead, and the EGO platform is outdoor-only, so the battery will not move into a drill or a work light.",
    "pros": [
      "Powerload reloads the spool with a button press instead of hand-winding",
      "Line IQ auto-feed keeps line at length without bumping",
      "Largest kit battery here by stored energy: 56V and 4.0Ah",
      "Telescopic carbon fiber shaft lets you set the length to your height",
      "EGO lists a 5-year tool and 3-year battery and charger warranty"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Not attachment-capable; EGO sells a separate multi-head system for that",
      "EGO batteries do not power hand tools like drills or saws",
      "The automatic head has more parts to keep clean than a plain bump head"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The EGO ST1623T is the best battery string trimmer for most yards: the most stored energy in its kit, an automatic feed head and button-press reloading, and it is the clear pick unless you already own another platform."
  },
  {
    "id": "milwaukee-3016-21st",
    "name": "Milwaukee M18 FUEL 3016-21ST QUIK-LOK String Trimmer Kit",
    "badge": "Best Attachment System",
    "category": "18V M18, Brushless, 14/16-Inch Swath, QUIK-LOK Powerhead, 8.0Ah Kit",
    "description": "If you already own Milwaukee M18 batteries, this is the trimmer that makes the most of them. The 3016-21ST kit pairs the M18 FUEL QUIK-LOK powerhead with a 16-inch string trimmer attachment, an M18 REDLITHIUM FORGE XC8.0 battery and the M18 and M12 rapid charger. Milwaukee lists a rear-mounted POWERSTATE brushless motor, a 6,200 RPM maximum, three speed settings plus a variable trigger, an adjustable 14-inch or 16-inch swath, and 0.080-inch or 0.095-inch line in an Easy Load head that holds up to 25 feet. Milwaukee claims more power than 31cc gas trimmers and says the tool is optimised for the XC8.0 pack, which matters: owners who run a small compact M18 battery report noticeably weaker results. The QUIK-LOK coupler is the long-term reason to buy it, since the same powerhead takes Milwaukee's pole saw, blower, brush cutter and edger attachments.",
    "pros": [
      "Kit includes the large FORGE XC8.0 pack the tool is built around",
      "QUIK-LOK powerhead accepts pole saw, blower, brush cutter and edger heads",
      "Three speeds let you trade power for runtime on light work",
      "Runs on the same M18 batteries as Milwaukee's drills and saws",
      "Easy Load head holds up to 25 feet of line"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Performance drops with compact M18 packs; budget for a large battery",
      "Bump-feed head, not automatic feed",
      "Expensive if you own no M18 batteries yet"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "For M18 owners it is the obvious buy, and its QUIK-LOK powerhead is the best reason on this list to choose an attachment system over a dedicated trimmer."
  },
  {
    "id": "dewalt-dcst972b",
    "name": "DEWALT 60V MAX DCST972B Brushless Attachment Capable String Trimmer (Tool Only)",
    "badge": "Best for FLEXVOLT Owners",
    "category": "60V MAX, Brushless, 15/17-Inch Swath, Attachment Capable, Tool Only",
    "description": "DEWALT now lists the DCST972X1 kit as discontinued, but the DCST972B bare tool remains on its site, and for anyone who already owns FLEXVOLT batteries that is the version to buy anyway. DEWALT lists a brushless motor, an adjustable 15-inch or 17-inch swath, the widest here, 0.080-inch dual line with support for 0.095-inch line, a hi and lo speed switch on a variable trigger, and a quick-load spool. It is attachment-capable through a universal coupler. The catch is the battery: this trimmer needs a 60V MAX FLEXVOLT pack, and ordinary 20V MAX batteries will not run it. FLEXVOLT packs do drop into DEWALT's 20V MAX tools, so the battery you buy for the trimmer is not stranded in the shed. If you own only 20V MAX packs, this is a battery purchase as well as a tool purchase, and you should price it that way.",
    "pros": [
      "Widest maximum swath on this list at 17 inches",
      "Universal attachment coupler for add-on heads",
      "FLEXVOLT packs also run DEWALT 20V MAX tools",
      "Hi and lo speed control for stretching runtime",
      "Accepts 0.095-inch line for heavier growth"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Tool only: no battery or charger in the box",
      "Will not run on standard 20V MAX batteries",
      "Kit version is listed as discontinued, so bundles may be old stock"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "A strong, wide-cutting trimmer if FLEXVOLT batteries are already in your garage, and a poor value if they are not."
  },
  {
    "id": "ryobi-p20190",
    "name": "Ryobi 18V ONE+ HP P20190 Brushless Whisper Series 15-Inch String Trimmer Kit",
    "badge": "Best Value Platform",
    "category": "18V ONE+, Brushless, 13-15-Inch Swath, 6.0Ah Kit",
    "description": "Ryobi ONE+ is the battery platform the largest number of homeowners already own, and the P20190 is the best reason to put those packs to work in the yard. Ryobi lists a brushless motor, an adjustable 13-inch to 15-inch swath and 0.080-inch line on a REEL EASY+ bump head with a speed-winder crank for reloading. The kit includes a 6Ah battery and charger, and Ryobi rates it for over an hour and 30 minutes on that pack. Ryobi also claims it is more powerful than a 21cc gas trimmer and 28 percent quieter than gas, which is the Whisper Series pitch. Narrowing the swath to 13 inches lightens the load on the motor, a sensible setting for routine edging along a driveway. The limits are the ones you would expect from 18 volts and 0.080-inch line: it is a lawn-maintenance tool, not a brush clearer, and owners who push it into thick, woody growth report slow going. If you want ONE+ attachments, Ryobi sells a separate attachment-capable model, the P20220.",
    "pros": [
      "Runs on the widely owned Ryobi ONE+ 18V battery platform",
      "Kit includes a 6Ah battery, rated for over 90 minutes",
      "Adjustable swath trades width for runtime",
      "Speed-winder crank makes reloading the bump head quicker",
      "Ryobi markets it as 28 percent quieter than gas"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "0.080-inch line limits it in thick, stalky weeds",
      "Not attachment-capable; that is the P20220",
      "Bump head only; no automatic feed"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The sensible buy for ONE+ owners with a maintained suburban lot, and good value even if the battery is your first Ryobi pack."
  },
  {
    "id": "makita-xru23sm1",
    "name": "Makita XRU23SM1 18V LXT Brushless 13-Inch String Trimmer Kit",
    "badge": "Lightest Brushless Pick",
    "category": "18V LXT, Brushless, 13-Inch Swath, 4.0Ah Kit",
    "description": "Weight is the spec that decides how a trimmer feels at the end of a long edge, and Makita's XRU23 is the lightest brushless option here. Dealer listings give 5.2 pounds without a battery and 6.6 pounds with the 4.0Ah pack, and Makita rates it for up to an hour on that battery. The kit includes the trimmer, a BL1840B 4.0Ah battery, a DC18SD charger and a Rapid Load bump-and-feed head. Makita lists two speed ranges, 0 to 4,500 and 0 to 6,000 RPM, and a 13-inch swath that Makita describes as suited to compact and precision trimming. That narrow swath is the trade: it takes more passes on long fence lines and it is not meant for heavy growth. But for a townhouse or a small lot with lots of beds and edges, and for anyone with an LXT drill already in the cupboard, it is easy on the arms and shares batteries with one of the biggest pro tool lines.",
    "pros": [
      "Lightest brushless trimmer here at 6.6 pounds with battery, per dealer listings",
      "Uses Makita 18V LXT batteries shared with a huge tool range",
      "Two speed ranges for runtime or power",
      "Kit includes 4.0Ah battery and charger",
      "Rated for up to an hour on the included pack"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "13-inch swath means more passes on long runs",
      "Not built for thick weeds or brush",
      "Line diameter is not given on the kit listing we checked"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The trimmer for small lots and anyone who finds full-size trimmers tiring, especially if Makita LXT batteries are already on the shelf."
  },
  {
    "id": "stihl-fsa-60-r",
    "name": "STIHL FSA 60 R Battery String Trimmer",
    "badge": "Best Dealer-Backed",
    "category": "36V AK System, 13.8-Inch Swath, AutoCut Head, Dealer Sold",
    "description": "STIHL's FSA 60 R suits buyers who want a dealer to set it up, service it and stock its parts. It runs on the STIHL AK battery system, rated 36 volts, which also powers STIHL's AK-series blowers, hedge trimmers and mowers. STIHL lists a 13.8-inch swath and, with the AutoCut C 6-2 head, standard 0.095-inch line with 0.080-inch as an option, and STIHL rates it for up to 25 minutes per charge with the AK 20 battery. The unit is sold bare or as a set with battery and charger through dealers, and dealer listings put the bare tool at roughly 7 pounds. The 25-minute rating is the lowest on this list, so a large lot means a second battery. The case for it is service: a STIHL dealer will show you how it works, and a part is a counter visit away rather than a mail order.",
    "pros": [
      "Sold, assembled and serviced by local STIHL dealers",
      "AK batteries shared with STIHL's homeowner battery range",
      "Runs 0.095-inch line on the AutoCut head",
      "Several compatible STIHL heads listed",
      "Light bare-tool weight per dealer listings"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Rated runtime of up to 25 minutes on the AK 20 is the shortest here",
      "Narrow 13.8-inch swath",
      "AK batteries do not run hand tools"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "Buy it for the dealer relationship and the STIHL head options, and plan on a second battery for anything bigger than a small lot."
  },
  {
    "id": "black-decker-lste525",
    "name": "BLACK+DECKER LSTE525 20V MAX EasyFeed String Trimmer/Edger",
    "badge": "Best Budget for Small Yards",
    "category": "20V MAX, 12-Inch Swath, 0.065-Inch Line, Trimmer/Edger, 2 x 1.5Ah Kit",
    "description": "Not every yard needs a 56-volt machine. The LSTE525 is a small, light trimmer for a small lawn, and it is honest about it. Retailer listings give a 12-inch swath, 0.065-inch line, two speed settings and the EasyFeed system, which advances line when you press a button instead of bumping the head on the ground. As the trimmer/edger name says, it converts for edging along walks and patios, and the kit ships with two 20V MAX 1.5Ah batteries and a charger, so one can charge while you use the other. The thin line and the small packs are the limits. Thin 0.065-inch line breaks more often against fences and concrete, and owners regularly report replacing spools sooner than they expected. It is not marketed as brushless. For a patio, a few beds and a short fence line it is enough, and it costs a fraction of anything else here.",
    "pros": [
      "Light and simple, suited to small yards and occasional use",
      "EasyFeed advances line with a button press",
      "Converts to edging mode for walks and patios",
      "Two batteries in the kit",
      "Least expensive way into a battery trimmer on this list"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Thin 0.065-inch line breaks easily on hard edges",
      "1.5Ah packs hold little energy each",
      "12-inch swath and low power rule out larger lawns"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "A sensible tool for a postage-stamp yard; anything bigger, or anything with real weeds, deserves one of the picks above it."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Battery String Trimmers",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-battery-string-trimmer/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the best battery string trimmer?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For most yards, the EGO Power+ ST1623T. Its 56V 4.0Ah kit battery holds the most energy of the kits we compared, the Line IQ head feeds line automatically, and Powerload reloads the spool with a button press. If you already own Milwaukee M18, Ryobi ONE+ or Makita LXT batteries, that brand's trimmer is usually the better value."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is a higher-voltage battery trimmer always more powerful?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Not always. Higher voltage helps a trimmer hold line speed under load, so 56V and 60V tools tend to cut thick growth more easily. But motor design and battery size matter too. Milwaukee claims its 18V M18 FUEL trimmer out-powers 31cc gas trimmers when run on its large XC8.0 pack, which shows how much the battery matters."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long does a battery string trimmer run on one charge?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Manufacturer ratings on this list range from up to 25 minutes for the STIHL FSA 60 R on an AK 20 battery to over 90 minutes for the Ryobi P20190 on its 6Ah pack. Ratings assume light work. High speed in thick, wet grass shortens runtime considerably, so treat ratings as best cases."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I use my drill battery in a string trimmer?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "If the trimmer is on the same platform, yes. Ryobi ONE+, Milwaukee M18, Makita LXT and DEWALT 20V MAX batteries move between drills and yard tools. The catch is capacity: a small drill pack will run the trimmer only briefly and may limit its power. Use the battery size the maker pairs with the trimmer in its kit."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What line size should a cordless string trimmer use?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Use what the manufacturer specifies for the head. Most 18-volt trimmers use 0.080-inch line, light-duty models use 0.065-inch, and higher-energy trimmers such as the EGO, Milwaukee, DEWALT and STIHL picks run 0.095-inch. Heavier line lasts longer against fences but needs more power to spin, so do not exceed the rated size."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is an attachment-capable trimmer worth it?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Only if you will use at least one other attachment. A powerhead with an edger or pole saw is cheaper and more compact than buying separate tools, and it runs on one battery. If you will only ever trim, a dedicated trimmer is lighter and better balanced, and it costs less than the powerhead version."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Should I buy a kit or the tool-only version?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Buy tool-only if you already own large enough batteries on that platform, because the battery is a large part of the kit price. Buy the kit if you are new to the platform, since kits are usually the cheapest way to get a battery and charger. Check that the kit battery is big enough for the trimmer."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: authorName(Astro.url.pathname),
    section: "Lawn & Yard Care",
    tags: [
      "battery string trimmer",
      "cordless string trimmer",
      "battery weed eater",
      "battery trimmer platforms",
      "brushless string trimmer",
      "lawn care"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10" style="background-image: radial-gradient(circle at 20% 20%, #38BDF8 0, transparent 45%), radial-gradient(circle at 80% 60%, #FF4500 0, transparent 40%);"></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Battery Only
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          Best Battery <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">String Trimmers</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          Seven cordless trimmers sorted the way you should actually shop for one: by the battery platform behind it, the energy in the pack, the line it runs and whether it can grow into more than a trimmer.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#ego-st1623t"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#top-picks"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            Pick Your Platform
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="11.6k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#platform-first" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. Start With Your Battery</a>
          <a href="#volts-amp-hours" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Volts, Amp-Hours, Watt-Hours</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. The 7 Picks</a>
          <a href="#brushless" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Brushless Motors</a>
          <a href="#line-and-feed" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Line Size and Feed Heads</a>
          <a href="#attachments" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Attachment-Capable Systems</a>
          <a href="#weight-balance" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Weight and Balance</a>
          <a href="#kit-vs-tool-only" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Kit vs Tool-Only</a>
          <a href="#match-the-yard" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Matching a Trimmer to the Yard</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          The best battery string trimmer is usually the one that runs on batteries already sitting on your shelf. The voltage printed on the box is only half the story, and the rest of the decision comes down to watt-hours, line size, the feed head and whether you want one powerhead or three separate tools.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="platform-first" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Start With the Battery You Already Own
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most buying guides for battery trimmers start with power. That gets the order wrong. A cordless string trimmer is a motor wrapped around somebody else's battery, and the battery is the most expensive part you will buy. If you already own two or three packs from one brand, the bare-tool version of that brand's trimmer usually costs far less than a kit from a rival, and it will share a charger, a shelf and a habit you already have.
          </p>

          <p>
            So the first question is not which trimmer is strongest. It is which batteries are already in your garage. A household with a Ryobi ONE+ drill, a Milwaukee M18 impact driver or a DEWALT 20V MAX saw is already partly invested, and switching platforms means buying a second battery ecosystem that will never talk to the first. Our <a href="/best-cordless-tool-brand/">cordless tool brand guide</a> covers that choice in more depth, and it is worth reading before you commit to a trimmer as your first tool on a new platform.
          </p>

          <p>
            Battery platforms split into two camps. Tool platforms such as Milwaukee M18, Makita LXT, DEWALT 20V MAX and FLEXVOLT, and Ryobi ONE+ run drills, saws and lights as well as yard tools. Outdoor platforms such as EGO 56V and STIHL AK are built for mowers, blowers and trimmers and do not reach the workbench. Outdoor platforms tend to deliver more energy per pack for yard work. Tool platforms win on flexibility, because the same pack goes from the trimmer to the drill on the same afternoon.
          </p>

