        <div id="volts-amp-hours" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Volts, Amp-Hours and Watt-Hours: Reading the Box Honestly
          </h2>

          <p>
            Voltage is the number every brand puts in the biggest type, and it is the least useful number on its own. Voltage describes electrical pressure, how hard the battery can push current through the motor. Amp-hours describe capacity, how long it can keep pushing. Neither tells you how much work a pack can do. Multiply them together and you get watt-hours, which is the closest thing on the label to a fuel-tank size, and it is the number worth comparing between brands.
          </p>

          <p>
            Labels also mix nominal and maximum voltage. A 20V MAX pack is an 18-volt nominal pack measured at its peak with no load, and DEWALT lists its 60V MAX packs at 54 volts nominal. Comparing an 18-volt tool to a 20V MAX tool is a like-for-like comparison wearing different marketing. When a label prints watt-hours directly, use that. When it does not, multiply the nominal voltage by the amp-hours.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Kit battery</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Calculation</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Approximate energy</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">EGO 56V 4.0Ah</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">56 x 4.0</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">224 Wh</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">DEWALT FLEXVOLT 3.0Ah at 60V MAX, 54V nominal</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">54 x 3.0</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">162 Wh</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Milwaukee M18 FORGE XC8.0</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">18 x 8.0</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">144 Wh</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Ryobi ONE+ 6Ah</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">18 x 6.0</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">108 Wh</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Makita LXT 4.0Ah</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">18 x 4.0</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">72 Wh</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">BLACK+DECKER 20V MAX 1.5Ah, each of two</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">18 x 1.5</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">27 Wh</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            That table explains more about real runtime than any voltage badge. A high-voltage motor can turn line at speed under load with less current, which is why 56-volt and 60-volt machines tend to hold speed better in thick growth. But an 18-volt trimmer fed a big pack can match them on endurance, and Milwaukee builds its trimmer around exactly that idea. For a deeper explanation of how cells, voltage and capacity relate, our explainer on <a href="/cordless-tool-batteries-explained/">cordless tool batteries</a> walks through it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Treat rated runtime figures as best cases. Manufacturers measure them at low speed in light grass, and the same trimmer on high speed in wet, dense growth may run a fraction as long. The useful comparison is between ratings from the same brand, or between watt-hours across brands. If your lot takes longer than one pack to trim, the fix is a second battery, not a bigger motor.
          </p>
        </div>

