        <div id="brushless" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Brushless Motors: What You Actually Get
          </h2>

          <p>
            Five of the seven trimmers here are listed with brushless motors, and that is not an accident. A brushed motor passes current through carbon brushes that rub against a spinning commutator. They wear, they make heat, and that friction wastes energy that could have gone into the line. A brushless motor replaces the brushes with electronics that switch the current, so more of each watt-hour turns into cutting and less into warmth.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a trimmer that efficiency shows up in three places. Runtime per charge improves, because less energy is lost inside the motor. The motor can sense load and draw more current when the line hits thick grass, which is why owners describe brushless trimmers as holding speed rather than bogging. And with no brushes to wear out, there is one less part that fails on an older tool. Our <a href="/brushless-vs-brushed-drill/">brushless vs brushed guide</a> explains the same trade in drills, and the logic carries straight over to yard tools.
          </p>

          <p>
            Brushless is not magic, though. A brushless motor on a small battery is still a small-battery trimmer, and a brushed budget tool can be perfectly adequate for a patio and a few beds. The BLACK+DECKER pick on this list is not marketed as brushless, and for its intended job that is fine. Where brushless earns its price is a medium or large lot, thicker weeds or regular use, which is where most buyers of a trimmer costing more than an entry-level model end up.
          </p>

          <p>
            One practical note: brushless trimmers almost always come with variable speed or a high and low switch. Use it. Running on low for routine edging around a lawn that is cut weekly can stretch a pack well past what the high-speed rating suggests, and it is easier on line as well.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="line-and-feed" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Line Size, Heads and Feed Systems
          </h2>

          <p>
            Line diameter is the most underrated spec on a battery trimmer. Thin 0.065-inch line is light, so a small motor can spin it fast, but it shreds against chain link and concrete. Mid-weight 0.080-inch line is the standard on most 18-volt trimmers and handles maintained lawns well. Heavier 0.095-inch line, which the EGO, Milwaukee, DEWALT and STIHL picks run, survives fence posts and stalky weeds far better, but it takes more power to swing, which is why it shows up on higher-energy tools.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not force heavier line into a head rated for less. It overloads the motor, cuts runtime and can stall the spool. If your trimmer is rated for 0.080-inch line, buy good 0.080-inch line, ideally a twisted or multi-sided profile, before you consider going thicker. Each product above lists what its manufacturer specifies, and the head label or manual is the final word.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Bump feed, button feed and automatic feed</h3>

          <p>
            Bump-feed heads release line when you tap the head on the ground while it spins. They are simple and reliable, and most trimmers use them, including the Milwaukee, Ryobi and Makita picks. Button feed, as on the BLACK+DECKER EasyFeed, releases line when you press a button on the handle, which spares the head from being knocked against the ground. Automatic feed, as on EGO's Line IQ head, senses line length and feeds it without any input.
          </p>

          <p>
            Reloading is a separate question from feeding, and it is where owners most often give up on a trimmer. EGO's Powerload winds line onto the spool with a button press, Milwaukee's Easy Load head and Ryobi's speed-winder crank let you load without taking the head apart, and STIHL's AutoCut head with EasySpool works the same way. If you are stuck with a head that has to come apart, our guide on <a href="/how-to-restring-a-weed-eater/">how to restring a weed eater</a> walks through every common head type step by step.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="attachments" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Attachment-Capable Systems: When One Powerhead Beats Three Tools
          </h2>

          <p>
            An attachment-capable trimmer splits into a powerhead, which holds the motor and battery, and a shaft with a tool on the end. Swap the string trimmer shaft for an edger, a pole saw, a hedge trimmer or a blower and the same battery and motor do a different job. Milwaukee calls its coupler QUIK-LOK, Ryobi calls its system EXPAND-IT, and DEWALT's DCST972B accepts universal attachments.
          </p>

          <p>
            The economics are simple. If you would otherwise buy a trimmer, a stick edger and a pole saw, a powerhead plus attachments usually costs less, needs one charger and takes less wall space. If a proper edge along a long driveway matters to you, an edger attachment on the same powerhead is a lot cheaper than a second dedicated machine. Our <a href="/best-lawn-edger/">lawn edger guide</a> covers when a dedicated edger is still worth it.
          </p>

          <p>
            The costs are real too. Attachment systems are usually a little heavier and slightly less balanced than a dedicated trimmer, because the coupler adds weight midway along the shaft. Swapping takes a minute, which matters if you edge and trim in the same pass. And attachments only fit their own system, so a QUIK-LOK edger will not fit an EXPAND-IT powerhead. Choose the system along with the battery platform, because you are buying into both.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Buy the attachment system only if you will use two attachments.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A powerhead that only ever carries a trimmer shaft is a heavier, more expensive trimmer. If a pole saw or edger is genuinely on your list for the next year, the system pays for itself. If not, a dedicated trimmer like the EGO or Ryobi pick is lighter and simpler.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="weight-balance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Weight, Balance and Why the Battery Changes Both
          </h2>

          <p>
            Weight figures on battery trimmers are slippery, because many listings quote the bare tool without the battery, some quote it with the battery, and a few quote the shipping weight of the box. When you compare, make sure you compare like with like. Makita's XRU23, for example, is listed at 5.2 pounds bare and 6.6 pounds with its 4.0Ah pack, and that 1.4-pound difference is all battery.
          </p>

          <p>
            Balance matters as much as total weight. On most battery trimmers the pack sits behind the rear grip and acts as a counterweight to the head, which is why a well-balanced trimmer can feel lighter than its weight. A bigger battery adds weight in the right place for balance but the wrong place for your forearm, so the 8.0Ah pack that gives Milwaukee its endurance also makes it a heavier tool to hold. The trade is unavoidable: energy has mass.
          </p>

          <p>
            Shaft length is the other half of comfort. A shaft that is too short makes taller users stoop, and a shaft that is too long pushes the head out in front where it is hard to control. EGO's telescopic shaft on the ST1623T addresses this directly, which is one reason it suits households where more than one person trims. If a trimmer feels awkward in the store, it will feel worse after twenty minutes.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you have shoulder or back trouble, prioritise weight and balance over power. A lighter 18-volt trimmer that you are happy to pick up beats a stronger one that stays on the hook. Use a shoulder strap if the trimmer accepts one, and hold the head parallel to the ground, letting the line tips do the cutting rather than forcing the head into the grass.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="kit-vs-tool-only" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Kit vs Tool-Only: Doing the Real Math
          </h2>

          <p>
            Nearly every trimmer here comes in two forms: a kit with a battery and charger, and a tool-only version, sometimes called a bare tool. The kit costs more because a large battery is genuinely expensive, often a big share of the kit price. If you already own compatible batteries, tool-only is usually the better deal, as long as the batteries you own are big enough to run the trimmer properly.
          </p>

          <p>
            That last point catches people out. A compact 2Ah pack from a drill kit will physically fit an 18-volt trimmer, but it holds a fraction of the energy the trimmer was designed around, and owners report short runs and weak cutting with small packs. Milwaukee says outright that its trimmer is optimised for the XC8.0 pack. Check what battery the manufacturer pairs with the trimmer in its own kit, and treat that as the minimum you want to run.
          </p>

          <p>
            The DEWALT pick is a special case. Its kit is listed as discontinued, and the tool-only version needs a 60V MAX FLEXVOLT pack that most 20V MAX owners do not have. If you own only 20V packs, add the cost of a FLEXVOLT battery and charger before comparing it with a kit from another brand. The reverse is also worth knowing: a FLEXVOLT pack will run your 20V MAX drill, so the battery is not wasted.
          </p>

          <p>
            For buyers starting from scratch, a kit is almost always the cheaper way to get a battery and charger, and buying the trimmer kit then adding tool-only blowers and mowers later is how most homeowners build a platform. If you plan to add a blower soon, look at combo kits too, and see our <a href="/best-cordless-leaf-blower/">cordless leaf blower guide</a> for the blowers on each of these platforms.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="match-the-yard" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Matching a Battery Trimmer to the Yard
          </h2>

          <p>
            A small lot under a quarter acre, with a patio, a few beds and short fence lines, does not need 56 volts. The Makita XRU23 or the BLACK+DECKER LSTE525 will do the job with less weight, and the narrow swath is less of a handicap when the runs are short. Choose the Makita if you want brushless efficiency and LXT batteries, and the BLACK+DECKER if the budget is tight and the use is light.
          </p>

          <p>
            A typical suburban lot of a quarter to half an acre is the sweet spot for the Ryobi P20190 and the EGO ST1623T. Both carry rated runtimes of an hour or more on their kit batteries, both use 15-inch or wider swaths, and the choice between them is mostly about platform. Ryobi wins if ONE+ batteries are already in the house. EGO wins if you are starting fresh or already run EGO's mower.
          </p>

          <p>
            Larger properties, overgrown edges and ditch banks call for 0.095-inch line and a big battery, which points to the EGO, the Milwaukee with its XC8.0 pack, or the DEWALT on a FLEXVOLT battery. Plan on a second pack for anything over half an acre. If a pole saw or edger is on your list, the Milwaukee QUIK-LOK system is the most complete attachment family here.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, think about who will service it. Big-box battery tools are usually replaced under warranty by mail or at the store. The STIHL FSA 60 R is the pick for buyers who would rather hand a tool to a local dealer and get it back working, and that relationship can matter more than a few minutes of rated runtime.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing voltage instead of watt-hours</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 56-volt badge and an 18-volt badge say little about how long each trimmer will run. Multiply nominal voltage by amp-hours, or read the watt-hour figure on the pack, and compare that. An 18-volt trimmer on an 8Ah pack holds more energy than many higher-voltage kits on small batteries.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running a big trimmer on a compact drill battery</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                It fits, so it seems fine, but a 2Ah drill pack holds a fraction of the energy the trimmer was designed around. Expect short runs and weak cutting. Use at least the battery size the manufacturer ships in its own kit, and keep compact packs for the drill.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying into a second battery platform by accident</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A great trimmer on the wrong platform means a second charger, a second set of batteries and packs that cannot move between tools. Check what you already own first. A bare tool on your existing platform often beats a better-reviewed kit from a brand you have never used.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forcing heavier line into a light-duty head</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Thicker line lasts longer, so it is tempting to load 0.095-inch line into a head rated for 0.080 or 0.065. It overloads the motor, shortens runtime and can jam the spool. Buy better line in the specified diameter, such as twisted or multi-sided profiles, instead of thicker line.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Paying for an attachment system you will not use</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A powerhead that only ever carries the trimmer shaft is a heavier, more expensive trimmer. Attachment systems pay off when you will genuinely use an edger, pole saw or blower head. If you will not, a dedicated trimmer is lighter, simpler and usually better balanced.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

