        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best battery string trimmer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most yards, the EGO Power+ ST1623T. Its 56V 4.0Ah kit battery holds the most energy of the kits we compared, the Line IQ head feeds line automatically, and Powerload reloads the spool with a button press. If you already own Milwaukee M18, Ryobi ONE+ or Makita LXT batteries, that brand's trimmer is usually the better value.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a higher-voltage battery trimmer always more powerful?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not always. Higher voltage helps a trimmer hold line speed under load, so 56V and 60V tools tend to cut thick growth more easily. But motor design and battery size matter too. Milwaukee claims its 18V M18 FUEL trimmer out-powers 31cc gas trimmers when run on its large XC8.0 pack, which shows how much the battery matters.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does a battery string trimmer run on one charge?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Manufacturer ratings on this list range from up to 25 minutes for the STIHL FSA 60 R on an AK 20 battery to over 90 minutes for the Ryobi P20190 on its 6Ah pack. Ratings assume light work. High speed in thick, wet grass shortens runtime considerably, so treat ratings as best cases.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use my drill battery in a string trimmer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                If the trimmer is on the same platform, yes. Ryobi ONE+, Milwaukee M18, Makita LXT and DEWALT 20V MAX batteries move between drills and yard tools. The catch is capacity: a small drill pack will run the trimmer only briefly and may limit its power. Use the battery size the maker pairs with the trimmer in its kit.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What line size should a cordless string trimmer use?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Use what the manufacturer specifies for the head. Most 18-volt trimmers use 0.080-inch line, light-duty models use 0.065-inch, and higher-energy trimmers such as the EGO, Milwaukee, DEWALT and STIHL picks run 0.095-inch. Heavier line lasts longer against fences but needs more power to spin, so do not exceed the rated size.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is an attachment-capable trimmer worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if you will use at least one other attachment. A powerhead with an edger or pole saw is cheaper and more compact than buying separate tools, and it runs on one battery. If you will only ever trim, a dedicated trimmer is lighter and better balanced, and it costs less than the powerhead version.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I buy a kit or the tool-only version?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Buy tool-only if you already own large enough batteries on that platform, because the battery is a large part of the kit price. Buy the kit if you are new to the platform, since kits are usually the cheapest way to get a battery and charger. Check that the kit battery is big enough for the trimmer.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

